unit in brackets, such as \code{"[m]"}, reads as the unit inside them. In
an OTN extract a sensor detection's code is \code{sensorName}, so \code{transmitter}
takes it, and \code{tag_name} keeps the pinger code OTN files it under.

\strong{Receivers.} A receiver written as model and serial, such as
\code{"VR2Tx-480013"}, is split: \code{receiver_serial} holds the serial,
\code{"480013"}, as deployment sheets write it, and \code{receiver_model} holds the
model. Only a known receiver family (VR, HR, TBR and others) followed by
three or more digits is split, so OTN collector numbers such as
\code{"HFX068-00348-1"} stay whole.

Columns the map does not name are kept after the contract columns, with
their names in lower case and underscores. No row is removed, except the
OTN release records and repeated VDAT records named above.

